Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to build a
Python script with the following specification: Create a Python 3 script for
Termux (Android) that automates creating a monthly git branch in a local
repository located at the user's home directory under a `bin` folder.
The script must begin with a Termux-specific shebang line pointing to the Termux
Python interpreter and use postponed annotation evaluation (`from __future__
import annotations`).
It should use only the standard library: `subprocess`, `sys`, `datetime`, and
`pathlib`.
Define module-level constants for the repository path (the `bin` directory
inside the user's home, resolved via `Path.home()`) and the main branch name
(`main`).
Implement these functions: 1. `get_branch_name() -> str`: returns the current
month's name in lowercase followed by an underscore and the four-digit year (for
example, a name of the form `<month>_<year>`), derived from the current local
date/time. 2. `branch_exists(branch_name: str) -> bool`: returns True if the
branch exists either locally or on the remote.
First run `git branch --list <name>` in the repository directory with captured
text output and check whether the name appears in stdout.
If not found locally, run `git ls-remote --heads origin <name>` in the same
directory and check whether the string `refs/heads/<name>` appears in stdout.
Wrap the whole check in a broad exception handler that returns False on any
error.
Use `capture_output=True` and `text=True` for all subprocess calls. 3.
`create_monthly_branch() -> bool`: compute the branch name, print `Creating
branch for <name>...`, and if the branch already exists print a success line
with a check mark (`✓ Branch '<name>' already exists`) and return True.
Otherwise, inside a try block: run `git fetch --all` (with `check=True` and
captured output), run `git checkout <MAIN_BRANCH>` (with `check=True` and
captured output), then pull the latest changes from the remote for the main
branch, create and switch to the new branch using `git checkout -b`, push it to
origin with upstream tracking (`git push -u origin <name>`), and print a success
message with a check mark on completion.
Return True on success.
Catch `subprocess.CalledProcessError` to print an error message that includes
the failing command's stderr (decoded safely, handling None), and return False.
Catch any other exception with a generic error message and return False. 4.
A `main()` function that calls `create_monthly_branch()` and exits with status 0
on success or 1 on failure using `sys.exit`, and an `if __name__ == "__main__":`
guard invoking it.
Behavioral requirements and edge cases to honor: the script must not create
duplicate branches if one already exists locally or remotely; it must operate
entirely within the repository directory via the `cwd` argument rather than
changing the process working directory; all git failures must be reported to the
user with useful stderr output rather than crashing with an unhandled traceback;
and the month name must be lowercase regardless of locale-specific
capitalization.
Output messages should be concise, plain text, and use the check mark symbol
only for success lines.
The script should be runnable directly as an executable file with no
command-line arguments required.
Deliver the complete script as a single file. ---
